---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "regions", label: "Regions" },
  { id: "panes", label: "Panes inside the scaffold" },
  { id: "scrolling", label: "Scrolling" },
  { id: "direction", label: "Right-to-left" },
  { id: "limits", label: "Limits" },
  { id: "accessibility", label: "Accessibility" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="scaffold" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A scaffold places an app's bars, rails and panes, so a page doesn't
          have to write its own shell grid. The components inside keep their
          own look and behavior; the scaffold only decides where each one goes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Put <code>.scaffold</code> on a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> that is a
          child of <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>. Not on <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>
          itself: the browser moves the body's overflow to the window, and the
          shell stops clipping. Its direct children are placed by what
          they are: the top app bar <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> spans the top,
          and the <a href="navigation-rail.html">navigation rail</a>,
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> and a vertical
          <a href="toolbars.html">toolbar</a> share the row below it, from the
          leading edge to the trailing one. The scaffold is as tall as the
          window and only <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> scrolls. The scaffold is
          CSS only.
        </p>
        <div data-markdown-ignore>
          <div class="scaffold" style="block-size: 26rem; border: 1px solid var(--md-sys-color-outline-variant)">
            <header>
              <div class="bar">
                <button type="button" aria-label="Main menu">
                  <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">menu</span>
                </button>
                <h2>Mail</h2>
              </div>
            </header>
            <nav class="navigation-rail" aria-label="Scaffold demo navigation">
              <a href="#anatomy" aria-current="page"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">inbox</span>Inbox</a>
              <a href="#regions"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>Starred</a>
              <a href="#scrolling"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">send</span>Sent</a>
            </nav>
            <div class="scaffold-pane" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="scaffold-demo-inbox">
              <div class="p-4">
                <h3 id="scaffold-demo-inbox">Inbox</h3>
                <p>The pane scrolls on its own. The bar, rail and toolbar stay where they are.</p>
                <p style="block-size: 40rem">Keep scrolling.</p>
                <p>End of the inbox.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="toolbar vertical">
              <button type="button" aria-label="Calendar"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">calendar_month</span></button>
              <button type="button" aria-label="Notes"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">sticky_note_2</span></button>
              <button type="button" aria-label="Tasks"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">task_alt</span></button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The demo gives the scaffold a fixed height and uses
          <code>.scaffold-pane</code>, because this page already has its own
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>. In an app, write it like this:
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="scaffold">
  <header>
    <a class="visually-hidden-focusable" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <div class="bar">
      <button type="button" aria-label="Main menu">
        <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">menu</span>
      </button>
      <h1>Mail</h1>
    </div>
  </header>
  <nav class="navigation-rail" aria-label="Primary">
    <a href="/inbox" aria-current="page"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">inbox</span>Inbox</a>
    <a href="/starred"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>Starred</a>
  </nav>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">…</main>
  <div class="toolbar vertical">
    <button type="button" aria-label="Calendar"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">calendar_month</span></button>
  </div>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.regions}>
        <p>Each region takes one direct child:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Bar: a <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> top app bar, or <code>.scaffold-bar</code>.</li>
          <li>Rail: a <code>.navigation-rail</code>, or <code>.scaffold-rail</code>.</li>
          <li>Pane: <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, or <code>.scaffold-pane</code>.</li>
          <li>Trailing: a <code>.toolbar.vertical</code> or <code>.toolbar-group.vertical</code>, or <code>.scaffold-trailing</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Use the region classes for custom content that isn't one of those
          components. A region with nothing in it takes no space: without a
          rail the pane meets the leading edge, and without a bar the rail and
          pane start at the top. A second child in the same region would
          overlap the first, so give the ones you aren't showing
          <code>hidden</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rail fills its region from the bar down to the bottom of the
          window and scrolls its own destinations when they don't fit.
          Expanding it widens its column and narrows the pane. The vertical
          toolbar is centered in its region, 24px from the pane and from the
          window edge.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="scaffold">
  <div class="scaffold-bar">…</div>
  <div class="scaffold-rail">…</div>
  <div class="scaffold-pane">…</div>
  <div class="scaffold-trailing">…</div>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.panes}>
        <p>
          Put a <a href="panes.html">panes</a> layout inside
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, or put the panes classes on
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> itself. The panes fill the pane region and
          each one scrolls on its own.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<main id="main" class="panes list-detail" tabindex="-1">
  <div class="list-pane">…</div>
  <div class="detail-pane">…</div>
</main>
`} />
        <p>
          Panes choose their columns from the window width. The pane region is
          narrower than the window by the rail and toolbar, so near 840px a
          list-detail layout can show two columns in a narrow region. To make
          the panes follow the region instead, set
          <code>container-type: inline-size</code> on <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>.
          That also makes <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> the containing block for
          anything fixed inside it, such as a FAB, which then scrolls with the
          pane.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.scrolling}>
        <p>
          The scaffold never scrolls the document. <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>
          scrolls, and so does a rail with more destinations than fit. Give
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> <code>tabindex="-1"</code> and a skip link
          that points at it, so keyboard users can move into the pane and
          scroll it with Page Down, Space and the arrow keys. Put the skip link
          inside the <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>: a direct child of the
          scaffold that isn't a region would land in a free grid cell.
        </p>
        <p>
          A top app bar fills with <code>surface-container</code> once the pane
          has scrolled, in browsers that support <code>timeline-scope</code>.
          Elsewhere it stays at rest. When a panes layout scrolls inside
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, the bar stays at rest too, since
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> itself doesn't scroll.
        </p>
        <p>
          Printing drops the fixed height, so the whole pane prints. The rail
          doesn't print.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.direction}>
        <p>
          The regions follow the writing direction. Under
          <code>dir="rtl"</code> the rail is on the right and the toolbar on
          the left.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.limits}>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <code>.medium</code> and <code>.large</code> app bars collapse when
            the window scrolls, so in a scaffold they stay expanded. Use the
            small bar.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="back-to-top.html">Back to top</a> watches the window, so
            in a scaffold it never appears.
          </li>
          <li>
            A navigation rail's side panel spans the window height, so it opens
            over the bar.
          </li>
          <li>
            A trailing toolbar taller than its region is cut off at the
            bottom, so keep it to the actions that fit. Custom
            <code>.scaffold-rail</code> and <code>.scaffold-trailing</code>
            content scrolls instead.
          </li>
          <li>
            Other children aren't placed. They fill whatever grid cell is free.
          </li>
        </ul>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.accessibility}>
        <p>
          The scaffold adds no landmark. Its <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> is the
          page's banner, the navigation rail is a navigation landmark, and
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> is the main landmark, in that order. Write
          the scaffold as a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, never as a landmark element or with a
          landmark role. A page has one scaffold; use panes or the grid inside
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> for more columns.
        </p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>
